   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to increasing\ninsurance limits for certain for-hire vehicles; and to amend part III of\nchapter 59 of the laws of 2019 amending the transportation law, the vehicle\nand traffic law and the insurance law relating to limousine safety, in\nrelation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5329",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5329-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §370, V & T L; amd Part III §20, Chap 59 of 2019",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5329\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to increasing insurance limits for certain for-hire vehicles;\nand to amend part III of chapter 59 of the laws of 2019 amending the\ntransportation law, the vehicle and traffic law and the insurance law\nrelating to limousine safety, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to make corrections to Chapter 59 of the laws\nof 2019 to include property damage limits within the new combined single\nlimits of coverage for certain vehicles.  Summary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1: Amends the vehicle and traffic law to include property\ndamages within the combined single limit for liability insurance cover-\nages of $1.5 million\n\nSection 2: Amends the effective date set forth in Section 20 of Part Ill\nof Chapter 59 of 2019 to clarify that new insurance limits apply to\npolices issued or renewed after the effective date of the law.\n\nSection 3. Sets for the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChapter 59 of the laws of 2019 established new minimum liability insur-\nance requirements for vehicles seating 8 or more passengers that are\nengaged in the business of carrying or transporting passengers for hire.\nThe new minimum liability limits are now a \"combined single limit\" as\nopposed to \"split limits\" of liability, which they were before Chapter\n59 was enacted. \"Split limits\" of liability coverage mean there are\nthree separate dollar amounts that apply to each accident: a per person\nbodily injury limit, a per occurrence limit for all injured persons, and\na per occurrence limit for all property damage resulting from the acci-\ndent.\n\nFor example, a vehicle seating 8-12 passengers previously had a per\nperson bodily injury limit of $25,000 and a per occurrence limit for two\nor more injured persons of $80,000, and $10,000 for property damage\nlimit per occurrence. These coverages or commonly referred to as\n25/80/10 split limits. (The law also provided for higher split limits of\n50/150/10 in the event injuries resulted in death of a person.)\n\nChapter 59 changed the split limit insurance requirements to a combined\nsingle liability limit requirement of $1.5 million. \"Combined single\nlimits\"\n\nstate a single dollar limit that applies to any combination of bodily\ninjury (or death) and property damage claims.\n\nHowever, Chapter 59 did not include the property damage minimum limits\nof $10,000 within the new combined single liability limits of $1.5\nmillion. Therefore, the new bodily injury (and death) limits are $1.5\nmillion dollars, but the property damage limits remain separate at\n$10,000.\n\n\nThe New York Automobile Insurance Plan (NYAIP) is the residual market\nfor motor vehicle risks, including commercial motor vehicles such as\nfor-hire vehicles. It was created by Article 53 of the Insurance Law to\nprovide motor vehicle insurance to those consumers who could not obtain\nit in the voluntary insurance market.\n\nThe NYAIP does not provide combined single liability limits of coverage\nthat do not include both bodily injury, death, and property damage.\nTherefore, it will be unable to provide the coverage the law requires\nand has no way to calculate rates for it either.\n\nAdditionally, with respect to the effective date for the new limits,\nChapter 59 provides that the new increased insurance limits become\neffective on January 1, 2020 and apply to all policies \"issued, renewed,\naltered, or modified\" on or after that date.\n\nTypically, when new insurance coverages are required by law, they apply\nto newly issued policies and those that renew after the effective date.\nChapter 59 goes a step further and would require the new limits of\ncoverage be applied even when a change is made to the policy.  This\ncould result in an insurer having to calculate and institute the higher\nlimits when a policyholder simply wants to change an address or add a\ndriver to the policy. Not only will it be very difficult for an insurer\nto process this type of mid-term coverage and premium change, but it\nwill surely catch most policyholders by surprise to see a dramatic\npremium increase during their policy period with no advance warning.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter 59 of 2019, Part III (S.1509-C and A.2009-C)\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect at the same time as Chapter 59 of 2019.\n",
